course: dictionary
lesson: 8660
title: Seawater: the salty sea
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Seawater is the salty water of the sea or the ocean. Read the words around it to see what it means: «A submarine fills tanks with seawater to sink and blows them out with air to rise.» Tanks, sink and rise are clues that seawater is water that a submarine can use to move up and down.
explain: The salt in seawater is not an extra ingredient added by the sea. Rivers wash tiny bits of salt out of rocks and carry them to the sea over millions of years. Salt stays behind when the water evaporates, which is how sea salt is made.
explain: Seawater is not safe to drink. «A gland above a penguin's eyes removes extra salt from its blood» because birds have a special gland to get rid of salt, but people do not, so drinking seawater makes a person thirstier and ill.
fact: Seawater is salty because it has salts dissolved in it, mostly common salt, which is sodium chloride.
fact: Rivers wash salts out of rocks and carry them to the sea over millions of years.
fact: There is about 35 grams of salt in every litre of seawater.
fact: Seawater is denser than fresh water, so it is easier to float in.
fact: Seawater has currents and tides, so swim between the flags where lifeguards watch.
exercise: What is seawater? | Fresh water from a river or lake | A kind of seaweed that grows on rocks | Water that has been heated in a kettle | *The salty water of the sea or the ocean
exercise: «A submarine fills tanks with seawater to sink.» What makes a submarine sink? | Sunshine on its deck | *Seawater filling its tanks | Air in its tanks | Fresh rain falling on it
exercise: «Every 10 metres of seawater presses as hard as the whole air above us.» What does this tell you about seawater? | It is warm, so it is comfy | *It is heavy, so it presses hard on divers | It is light, so it floats away | It is sweet, like lemonade
test: Why can you not drink seawater? | It has too much sugar in it | It has no taste at all | *It has salt in it, which makes you thirstier and ill | It turns into rain when you swallow it
test: How is sea salt made from seawater? | By boiling it in a pan for one minute | By sieving it through sand | *By evaporation, when the water dries up into the air | By freezing it in the snow
exam: Why do sea turtles seem to cry salty «tears»? | *Glands near their eyes push out the extra salt from the seawater they swallow | They have been rubbing sand in their eyes | They are sad about the waves | They have been eating sweets
